            <rel name="nonvolitional-cause" type="rst"/>
            <rel name="summary" type="rst"/>
            <rel name="conjunction" type="multinuc"/>
        </relations>
    </header>
    <body>
        <segment id="1" parent="2" relname="circumstance">When I read this article,</segment>
        <segment id="2" parent="23" relname="span">it made me think of something that happened at work recently.</segment>
        <segment id="3" parent="26" relname="span">A programmer who "knew what the user wanted" created a web page for payroll and other type of file submissions to the bank.</segment>
        <segment id="4" parent="5" relname="concession">He put security on the files,</segment>
        <segment id="5" parent="25" relname="span">but there was no security if one went through the web page to submit the files to the bank.</segment>
        <segment id="6" parent="24" relname="span">What happened was that a user who didn't have access to the payroll file was going through the web page and accidentially hitting the payroll submission button to submit the file.</segment>
        <segment id="7" parent="22" relname="span">Payroll was being submitted twice to the bank.</segment>
        <segment id="8" parent="21" relname="span">What this tells me is that the users reallly needed protection from errors.</segment>
        <segment id="9" parent="27" relname="conjunction">The users were never exposed to the web page prior to being put in production,</segment>
        <segment id="10" parent="27" relname="conjunction">and the programmer based his design on his own discretion.</segment>
        <segment id="11" parent="27" relname="conjunction">He was familiar with guidelines</segment>
        <segment id="12" parent="27" relname="conjunction">and felt that this would fit the bill.</segment>
        <segment id="13" parent="20" relname="span">I am not knocking the programmer</segment>
        <segment id="14" parent="13" relname="evidence">(he really is very talented and just made an error in judgement...</segment>
        <segment id="15" parent="20" relname="evidence">and he was probably pressed for time),</segment>
        <segment id="16" parent="29" relname="span">but I see that not applying usability concepts to the little things can lead to big problems.</segment>
        <group id="17" type="span"/>
        <group id="18" type="span" parent="17" relname="span"/>
        <group id="19" type="span" parent="16" relname="concession"/>
        <group id="20" type="span" parent="19" relname="span"/>
        <group id="21" type="span" parent="18" relname="span"/>
        <group id="22" type="span" parent="28" relname="span"/>
        <group id="23" type="span" parent="3" relname="background"/>
        <group id="24" type="span" parent="7" relname="nonvolitional-cause"/>
        <group id="25" type="span" parent="6" relname="nonvolitional-cause"/>
        <group id="26" type="span" parent="22" relname="nonvolitional-cause"/>
        <group id="27" type="multinuc" parent="8" relname="evidence"/>
        <group id="28" type="span" parent="21" relname="nonvolitional-cause"/>
        <group id="29" type="span" parent="18" relname="summary"/>
    </body>
